Wait for the Sensor Core instead of spinning on it.

ssc_common_wait_sync_context() drives the default GLib context with
g_main_context_iteration(..., FALSE).  When may_block is FALSE, GLib forces the
poll timeout to zero, so the loop is not a wait at all: it is a spin that runs
as fast as the CPU allows.  Any request the Sensor Core does not answer
therefore pins a whole core for as long as the process lives.

Measured on the X910 (SM8550, kernel 7.2-rc3): iio-sensor-proxy 3.9 stuck in
ssc_sensor_light_open_sync() burned 100 % of one core from boot, 35 000 loop
iterations per second, 106 941 ppoll() calls in three seconds, all with
{tv_sec=0, tv_nsec=0} and all returning 0 (Timeout), 99,64 % of process time.
That cost ~90 degrees on the hottest thermal zone at idle and about a third of
the charging current.

Blocking in poll() costs nothing in behaviour: the completion callback is
dispatched from this very context, so whatever wakes the poll is what ends the
wait, and every other source -- D-Bus, the accelerometer stream -- keeps being
dispatched from the nested iteration exactly as before.

Only the thread that drives the context may block on it, so acquire it first
and fall back to the condition variable the callback already signals for a
caller on any other thread.

diff --git a/src/libssc-common.c b/src/libssc-common.c
index 3648f47..3fbca7a 100644
--- a/src/libssc-common.c
+++ b/src/libssc-common.c
@@ -50,13 +50,39 @@ ssc_common_init_sync_context (SyncContext *ctx) {

 void
 ssc_common_wait_sync_context (SyncContext *ctx) {
+	GMainContext *context = g_main_context_default ();
+	gboolean owner;
+
+	/*
+	 * Iterating the context without blocking makes this a spin rather than a
+	 * wait: GLib forces the poll timeout to zero whenever may_block is FALSE,
+	 * so the loop runs as fast as the CPU allows.  A request the Sensor Core
+	 * never answers therefore pins a whole core for the life of the process.
+	 *
+	 * Block in poll() instead.  The completion callback is dispatched from
+	 * this same context, so whatever wakes the poll also ends the wait, and
+	 * every other source keeps being dispatched meanwhile exactly as before.
+	 *
+	 * Only the thread that drives the context may block on it.  A caller on
+	 * any other thread sleeps on the condition the callback already signals,
+	 * instead of fighting the owner for iterations.
+	 */
+	owner = g_main_context_acquire (context);
+
 	g_mutex_lock (&ctx->mutex);
 	while (!ctx->finished) {
-		g_mutex_unlock (&ctx->mutex);
-		g_main_context_iteration (g_main_context_default (), FALSE);
-		g_mutex_lock (&ctx->mutex);
+		if (owner) {
+			g_mutex_unlock (&ctx->mutex);
+			g_main_context_iteration (context, TRUE);
+			g_mutex_lock (&ctx->mutex);
+		} else {
+			g_cond_wait (&ctx->condition, &ctx->mutex);
+		}
 	}
 	g_mutex_unlock (&ctx->mutex);
+
+	if (owner)
+		g_main_context_release (context);
 }

 void
